PLAIN-SPEAK QUESTION

A40 showed that strong veteran cells
can survive a bad pulse longer,
but the population still flips.

A41 asks whether those veterans
can protect the neighborhood around them.

Not by forcing neighbors to agree.

Instead,
a strong surviving C core
makes it harder for a nearby uncertain cell
to be recruited into S during a short S pulse,
and vice versa.

The control asks whether
any nearby committed crowd is enough.

The main arm asks whether
old, well-supported veterans
need to count more than fresh commitments.

The test is:

CAN A SURVIVING CORE STOP
THE OPPOSING POPULATION
FROM REBUILDING AROUND IT
FAST ENOUGH TO CLOSE THE OSCILLATION CYCLE?

